Your Dockerfile Execution Flow:
Base Image: Starts with eclipse-temurin:21-jdk-jammy (JDK 21)

Workdir: Sets /app as working directory

Prepares Maven:

dockerfile
COPY .mvn .mvn
COPY mvnw pom.xml ./
RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y dos2unix && dos2unix mvnw && chmod +x mvnw
Copies Maven wrapper files

Ensures proper line endings for Unix environment

Makes Maven wrapper executable

Dependency Resolution:

dockerfile
RUN ./mvnw dependency:go-offline -B
Pre-downloads dependencies for offline builds

Source Code:

dockerfile
COPY src src
Copies your application source code

Build:

dockerfile
RUN ./mvnw clean package -DskipTests
Compiles and packages your Spring Boot application (skipping tests)

Configuration:

dockerfile
EXPOSE 8081
RUN addgroup --system spring && adduser --system --ingroup spring spring
USER spring:spring
Declares port 8081

Creates a non-root user for security

Entrypoint:

dockerfile
ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar", "target/VaccineManagementSystem-0.0.1-SNAPSHOT.jar"]
Defines how to run your application

Key Points:
Build Context: The entire ImmunizationTracker directory is sent to Docker daemon (because of the . in build command)

Layer Caching: Each instruction creates a layer that's cached for faster subsequent builds

Artifact Flow:

Your Jenkins pipeline first creates a JAR file (mvn package)

Then Docker build creates an image containing this JAR

Finally, the image is pushed to Docker Hub

Multi-Stage Benefit: While your current Dockerfile is single-stage, you could optimize it further by:

Using a multi-stage build to reduce final image size

Separating build dependencies from runtime environment
